          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            An ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triple"} spectral triple
            is, as discussed there, the abstract algebraic data characterizing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetric+quantum+mechanics"} supersymmetric quantum mechanics
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            and thereby spectrally encoding an effective (possibly
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-commutative+geometry"} non-commutative
            = succeed "\u201D)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-geometric+background"} non-geometric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+geometry"} Riemannian geometry
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin structure
            is the special case of this where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            in the spectral triple is that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/square+integrable+function"} square integrable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor+bundle"} spinor bundle
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            and the operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            acting on that is the standard
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinning+particle"} spinning particle
          %p
            In generalization of this, a “2-spectral triple” should be the analogous algebraic data that encodes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            theory of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            propagating on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            geometry which is a generalization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+geometry"} Riemannian geometry
            with (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+string+structure"}> twisted
            )
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+structure"} string structure
          %p
            Of course such data is just that of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+superconformal+field+theory"} 2d superconformal field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+operator+algebra"} vertex operator algebra
            or by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+net"} conformal net
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net+of+observables"} local observables
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triples"} spectral triples
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+system"} quantum system
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supercharge"} supercharge
                %th formalization
                %th algebra
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  quantum
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinning+particle"} spinning particle
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triple"} spectral triple
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebra"} operator algebra
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  quantum
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinning+string"} spinning string
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac-Ramond+operator"} Dirac-Ramond operator
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFT"} 2d SCFT
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+operator+algebra"} vertex operator algebra
          %p
            That the 0-mode sector of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFT"} 2d SCFT
            – hence the quantum point
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
            limit of a quantum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            dynamics – yields a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triple"} spectral triple
            was maybe first highlighted in (
            %a{:href => "#FroehlichGawedzki93"}> Fröhlich-Gawędzki 93
            ) by way of a series of concrete examples, such as the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW model
          %p
            Here the role of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
            of the spectral triple is played by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac-Ramond+operator"} Dirac-Ramond operator
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index"} index
            (in the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+volume+limit"} large volume limit
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten+genus"} Witten genus
          %p
            That hence the superstring quantum theory should be regarded as a kind of higher spectral triple was maybe first suggested in (
            %a{:href => "#Chamseddine97"}> Chamseddine 97
            ), together with arguments that the associated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+action"} spectral action
            indeed reproduces the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            of the string’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quantum+field+theory"} effective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            theory. An exposition of this perspective is in (
            %a{:href => "#FroehlichGrandjeanRecknagel97"}> Fröhlich-Grandjean-Recknagel 97, section 7.2
            ).
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory#Froehlich92"} Fröhlich 92, p. 11
          %blockquote
            %p
              I still have hopes, perhaps romantic ones, that
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℱ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebra+factor"}
                hyperfinite type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi III
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} III_1
                von Neumann algebras
              – to be a little technical –
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+net"} indexed by
              intervals of the circle with non-empty complement (or of the super-circle). It may pay to formulate the starting point using the language of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"} sheaves
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              This structure determines a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided monoidal
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-category"} C*-category
              with unit, …; briefly, a quantum theory. From a combination of such
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+categories"} tensor categories
              (left and right movers) one would attempt to reconstruct (symmetries of)
              = succeed "." do
                %em physical space-time
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} arrows
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intertwiners"}> intertwiners
              ) of the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+category"} tensor category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              it would provide a general way of thinking about string theory that does not presuppose knowing the target space-time of the theory.
          %p
            Later it was shown more formally (
            %a{:href => "#RoggenkampWendland03"}> Roggenkamp-Wendland 03
            ), reviewed in (
            %a{:href => "#RoggenkampWendland08"}> Roggenkamp-Wendland 08
            ), that there is a precise algebraically formalization of taking the “point particle limit” of a quantum string, by sending its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+operator+algebra"} vertex operator algebra
            to a spectral triple obtained by suitably retaining only
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            0-modes.
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Soibelman11"} Soibelman 2011
            used this as a means to systematically study the large volume limit of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quantum+field+theory"} effective
            string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            (and hence aspects of the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape of string theory vacua
          %p
            Now, since there is information lost in passing from a stringy “2-spectral triple” (a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFT"} 2d SCFT
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triple"} spectral triple
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-completion"} UV-completion
            of the corresponding target space
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theories"} effective field theories
          %p
            In view of this, it is noteworthy that the spectral triple of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Connes-Lott-Chamseddine+model"} Connes-Lott-Chamseddine model
            shares a few key properties with the 2d SCFTs considered in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+phenomenology"} string phenomenology
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Connes-Lott-Chamseddine+model"} Connes-Lott-Chamseddine model
            is an encoding in a spectral triple of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            coupled to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            realized as a kind of spectral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+compactification"} Kaluza-Klein compactification
            on a non-commutative fiber space down to ordinary 4d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            (or possibly its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+rotation"} Wick rotated
            Euclidean version). In order for this to work out, it turns out that the compactified non-commutative fiber space needs to have
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KO-dimension"} KO-dimension
            equal to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 6
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 6
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KO-dimension"} KO-dimension
          %p
            Hence the claim of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Connes-Lott-Chamseddine+model"} Connes-Lott-Chamseddine model
            is that if the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            is encoded as a singular limit of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+compactification"} Kaluza-Klein compactification
            modeled via a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triple"} spectral triple
            then the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimensions"} dimensions
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactification
            are
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 4
                  %mo +
                  %mn 6
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi mod
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mn 8
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  4 + 6 \;\;\; (mod\;8)
          %p
            with 4-dimensional base space and 6-dimensional fiber space, to a total of a 10-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            at high energy (after uncompactification of the fiber).
          %p
            This, of course, is precisely the dimensionality of the target spacetime of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory+vacua"} perturbative string theory vacua
            for the critical
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
          %p
            This point was highlighted in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Connes06"} Connes 06, p. 8
          %blockquote
            %p#Connes06OnRelationToStringVacua
              When one looks at the table (7.2) of Appendix 7 giving the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KO-dimension"} KO-dimension
              of the finite space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              i.e. the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactification
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              one then finds that its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KO-dimension"} KO-dimension
              is now equal to 6
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modulo"} modulo
              8 (!). As a result we see that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KO-dimension"} KO-dimension
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} product space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo ×
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \times F
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              i.e. of 4d
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactification
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              is in fact equal to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 10
                    %mo ∼
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 10  \sim 2
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modulo"} modulo
              8. Of course the above 10 is very reminiscent of string theory, in which the finite space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              might bea good candidate for an “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"}> effective
              ”
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} compactification
              at least for low energies. But 10 is also 2
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modulo"} modulo
              8 which might be related to the observations of
              %a{:href => "#LauscherReuter06"} Lauscher-Reuter 06
              about
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
          %p
            Algebraically, this arises from the fact that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST+complex"} BRST complex
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            theory is consistent (has BRST differential squaring to 0) precisely if the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFT"} 2d SCFT
            has conformal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/central+charge"} central charge
            15, and each spacetime dimension contributes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \tfrac{1}{2}
            to this central charge (a contribution of 1 from each bosonic direction, and another
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tfrac{1}{2}
            for the corresponding fermionic contribution).
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#flop_transition Flop transition
          %p
            There is at least evidence that there is a continuous path in the space of 2-spectral triples that starts and ends at a point describing the ordinary geometry of a complex 3-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+space"} Calabi-Yau space
            but passes in between through a 2-spectral triple/2d SCFT (a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gepner+model"} Gepner model
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flop+transition"} flop transition
            (alluding to the fact that the geometries at the start and end of this path have different
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+volume+limit"} large volume limit
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacua"} vacua
            in (
            %a{:href => "#Soibelman11"}> Soibelman 11
            ).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triple"} spectral triple
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+action"} spectral action
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane+geometry"} D-brane geometry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+of+a+2-spectral+triple"} automorphism of a 2-spectral triple
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/swampland"} swampland
          %h2#References References
          %p
            An early observation that the 0-mode sector of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFT"} 2d SCFT
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triple"} spectral triple
          %ul
            %li#FroehlichGawedzki93
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J%C3%BCrg+Fr%C3%B6hlich"}> Jürg Fröhlich
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Krzysztof+Gaw%C4%99dzki"} Krzysztof Gawędzki
              = succeed "," do
                %em Conformal Field Theory and Geometry of Strings
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9310187"} arXiv:hep-th/9310187
          %p
            The suggestion to understand, conversely, the
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFT"} 2d SCFT
            as a higher spectral triple is due to
          %ul
            %li#Chamseddine97
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ali+Chamseddine"}> Ali Chamseddine
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em An Effective Superstring Spectral Action
              %strong 56
              (1997) 3555-3567 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9705153"}> arXiv:hep-th/9705153
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.56.3555"} doi:10.1103/PhysRevD.56.3555
          %p
            which claims to show that the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+action"} spectral action
            reproduces the correct effective background action known in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
          %ul
            %li#FroehlichGrandjeanRecknagel97
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J%C3%BCrg+Fr%C3%B6hlich"}> Jürg Fröhlich
              , Oliver Grandjean,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+Recknagel"} Andreas Recknagel
              = succeed "," do
                %em Supersymmetric quantum theory, non-commutative geometry, and gravitation
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9706132"} arXiv:hep-th/9706132
          %p
            A more formal derivation of how ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triples"} spectral triples
            arise as point particle limits of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+operator+algebra"} vertex operator algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFTs"} 2d SCFTs
            then appears in
          %ul
            %li#RoggenkampWendland03
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Roggenkamp"}> Daniel Roggenkamp
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katrin+Wendland"} Katrin Wendland
              = succeed "," do
                %em Limits and Degenerations of Unitary Conformal Field Theories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0308143"} arXiv:hep-th/0308143
          %p summarized in
          %ul
            %li#RoggenkampWendland08
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Roggenkamp"}> Daniel Roggenkamp
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katrin+Wendland"} Katrin Wendland
              = succeed "," do
                %em Decoding the geometry of conformal field theories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0803.0657"} arXiv:0803.0657
          %p
            A brief indication of some ideas of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yan+Soibelman"} Yan Soibelman
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
            on this matter is at
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2007/06/had_the_pleasure_of_talking.html"}
                %em Spectral triples and graph field theory
          %p
            Further development of this and application to the study of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+volume+limit"} large volume limit
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacua"} vacua
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Soibelman11
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yan+Soibelman"}> Yan Soibelman
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Collapsing CFTs, spaces with non-negative Ricci curvature and nc-geometry
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"} Hisham Sati
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
              (eds.),
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Mathematical+Foundations+of+Quantum+Field+and+Perturbative+String+Theory"} Mathematical Foundations of Quantum Field and Perturbative String Theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.00896"} arXiv:2506.00896
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/pspum-83"} ams:pspum-83
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.ksu.edu/~soibel/nc-riem-3.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/schreiber/files/SoibelmanCFTandRicciCurvature.pdf"} pdf
          %p also re-posted as:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yan+Soibelman"}> Yan Soibelman
              \:
              %em Moduli space of Conformal Field Theories and non-commutative Riemannian geometry
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.00896"}> arXiv:2506.00896
              \&amp;rbrack;
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (text of
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a{:href => "#Soibelman11"} Soibelman 2011
          %p based on
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"}> Maxim Kontsevich
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yan+Soibelman"} Yan Soibelman
              = succeed "," do
                %em Homological mirror symmetry and torus fibrations
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0011041"} arXiv:math/0011041
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/536540/"} spire
          %p
            (discussing aspects of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+mirror+symmetry"} homological mirror symmetry
          %p
            Analogous detailed discussion based not on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+operator+algebra"} vertex operator algebra
            description of local
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
            but on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            description via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+nets"} conformal nets
            is in
          %ul
            %li#CHKL09
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sebastiano+Carpi"}> Sebastiano Carpi
              , Robin Hillier,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yasuyuki+Kawahigashi"} Yasuyuki Kawahigashi
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roberto+Longo"} Roberto Longo
              = succeed "," do
                %em Spectral triples and the super-Virasoro algebra
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0811.4128"} arXiv:0811.4128
          %p
            where
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFTs"} 2d SCFTs
            are related essentially to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+nets"} local nets
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triples"} spectral triples
          %p Exposition of these results is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://www.physicsforums.com/insights/spectral-standard-model-string-compactifications/"} Spectral Standard Model and String Compactifications
          %p
            See also the references at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+model+for+elliptic+cohomology"} geometric model for elliptic cohomology
          %p
            That the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Connes-Lott+models"} Connes-Lott models
            could be
            = succeed "-limits" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory+vacua"} perturbative string theory vacua
            is also mentioned in
          %ul
            %li#Connes06
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
              , p. 8 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em Noncommutative Geometry and the standard model with neutrino mixing
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0608226"} arXiv:hep-th/0608226
        .revisedby
